  "manual_review": {
    "positive_findings": [
      "The candidate uses rights-reviewed botanical trunk, branch, twig, and leaf geometry rather than another generated scaffold.",
      "Three materially and geometrically distinct forms are represented across the exact current source population.",
      "Envelope normalization makes the imported forms comparable to the production broadleaf proxy without mutating donor packages.",
      "The source materials, Nanite settings, grounded pivots, provenance, and license chain remain intact for this baseline."
    ],
    "rejection_reasons": [
      "Near and mid-field foliage resolves as very dark, narrow masses instead of the broad, dense, rounded California interior-live-oak crowns required by the reference target.",
      "Several branch and leaf clusters break into bright, empty, or sparse artifacts, especially at Chili Bar, Meat Grinder, and Coloma.",
      "Far views still collapse into repeated vertical black marks and do not establish a continuous, distance-stable riparian canopy.",
      "The donor trees are generic wind-sculpted coastal broadleaf analogs, not reviewed Quercus wislizeni forms or an approved South Fork species set.",
      "Component-level selection repeats one form inside each HISM batch and does not provide the per-instance age, silhouette, seasonal, root, and understory variation needed for production.",
      "Terrain, water, other foliage, lighting, and shoreline integration remain synthetic; none of these frames is acceptable release media."
    ],
    "decision": "Reject v213 visual promotion. Retain the hash-locked CC0 donor intake and reversible scale-normalized integration seam as technical infrastructure only.",
    "next_required_visual_input": "Use the real donor geometry only in a distinct material/transition bracket that fixes masked leaf and branch-card behavior, calibrated two-sided foliage lighting, alpha-covered mips, and near/mid/far representation. Promotion still requires multiple reviewed California live-oak age forms, per-instance variation, roots/understory integration, temporal evidence, and named art/ecology approval."
